Coastal wetlands, including salt marshes, mangroves, and swamps, act as natural buffers against storm surges and high winds. Their ability to mitigate flood damage is rooted in simple but powerful physical principles involving friction, absorption, and energy dissipation.

The primary mechanism is **friction**. Dense vegetation—such as the roots of mangroves or the reeds in salt marshes—creates significant drag on moving water. As storm-driven waves and surge waters push inland, they must navigate through this complex, tangled matrix of plant stems and roots. This interaction slows the water’s velocity, effectively reducing its kinetic